#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:2
msgid "Creating a Release"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:5
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:7
msgid "About ten minutes to half an hour of free time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:9
msgid ""
"The main branch in a clean and buildable state, full of commits you are "
"proud of and know the users will love. This is of course the default state "
"at any given time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:11
msgid ""
"A normal computer (real or virtual) that has a command line and can run bash "
"scripts. Macs and Linux boxes are good choices here. If you know what you're "
"doing I'm sure it's entirely possible to do it on Windows as well - but then "
"you're on your own. In a pinch you can use ``secure.syncthing.net`` as it "
"has all the required tools installed, although you'll need to add your git "
"config, keys etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:13
msgid ""
"The source repo, Git, Go, and GPG. It doesn't much matter which version of "
"Go, we are not going to be building the release artifacts with it. If you "
"can build the project you are good to go. If you can't build it, please "
"don't attempt to make a release of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:15
msgid ""
"Push access to the repo, being able to bypass the pull request requirements. "
"This means ``admin`` or ``maintainers`` group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:17
msgid "An account on ``secure.syncthing.net`` with sudo access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:19
msgid "A TeamCity account on ``build.syncthing.net`` with deploy access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:22
msgid "Release Procedure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:24
msgid ""
"The procedure differs slightly depending on whether we're doing a release "
"candidate or a stable release. Candidate releases require work to prepare "
"the changelog, which will just be reused for the stable release. The stable "
"release on the other hand requires a slightly different release process and "
"is announced more widely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:27
msgid "Release Candidates - Write a Change Log"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:29
msgid ""
"Most of the change log is machine generated from the closed issues. We do "
"however need to make sure that issues belong to the correct milestone, have "
"the correct labels, and that the issue subject makes sense as a line in the "
"change log. To our help we have the purpose written tool `grt <https://"
"github.com/syncthing/github-release-tool>`__. The grt tool requires your "
"GitHub token to manage milestones and issues; you set the environment "
"variable ``GITHUB_TOKEN`` while you are working on the release (but "
"hopefully not all the time - programs can and do steal environment data)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:31
msgid ""
"To ensure that all closed issues are tagged with the milestone for the "
"release you are doing, use the following command. First, find the commit "
"hash or tag of the last commit on the *previous* release - changes since "
"this point is what we are going to consider part of this release. If there "
"haven't been any special releases or branching you can simply use the "
"previous release as the starting point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:33
msgid "$ grt milestone v0.14.50 --from=v0.14.49"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:37
msgid ""
"Visit the milestone in your browser and double check the issue subjects and "
"labels. Remember that only closed issues (not pull requests) will appear in "
"the change log. If there are specific things to note about this release, "
"such as changed APIs or config formats, briefly describe these changes in "
"the notes field. You can preview the change log using grt:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:39
msgid ""
"$ grt changelog v0.14.50\n"
"Bugfixes:\n"
"\n"
" - #5063: panic: cannot start already running folder\n"
" - #5073: lib/logger: tests fail due to compilation error with go 1.11"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:47
msgid ""
"In principle the output should be a complete, valid release note for the "
"release in question. Pipe it to a text file. If you're preparing a release "
"candidate you should specify the full candidate tag (e.g. v0.14.50-rc.1) to "
"the command above to get the correct title on the changelog."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:49
msgid "Add further notes or commentary to taste, if required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:52
msgid "Stable Release - Write a Change Log"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:54
msgid ""
"Use the change log from the corresponding release candidate, just change the "
"version in the first line and the first sentence that describes the release "
"status."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:57
msgid "Prepare the Release Branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:59
msgid ""
"Releases come from the ``release`` branch. If you are making a new candidate "
"release you will want to fast forward ``release`` to point at current "
"``main`` ``HEAD``. If you are making a stable release from the latest RC the "
"``release`` branch is already in the right place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:61
msgid ""
"$ git checkout release\n"
"$ git merge --ff-only main"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:66
msgid ""
"If there's been some funky business with the ``release`` branch and it can't "
"be fast forwarded to ``main``, 1) the previous release manager screwed up, "
"2) don't do a merge, just reset the branch to the right place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:68
msgid "Don't push the branch yet, we want to create the tag first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:71
msgid "Create and Push the Tag"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:73
msgid ""
"Make sure you push the tag before the release branch, as the latter "
"currently triggers the release job on the CI (and requires the tag already "
"being in place)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:76
msgid ""
"$ git tag -a -s -F ~/changelog.txt v0.14.50\n"
"$ git push --tags\n"
"$ git push origin release:release"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:82
msgid "The changelog file is the one you prepared previously."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:84
msgid ""
"You will need your PGP key at hand for this step. It should be your personal "
"PGP key, whatever you would normally use. If you don't have one you'll need "
"to create one for the purpose. Keep it around, keep it secure, upload the "
"public part to a key server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:86
msgid ""
"If your remote spec is nondefault, tailor the push command to suit. We "
"deliberately pushed the tags before the release branch, because the builder "
"may start building the release branch immediately and needs to see the right "
"tags at that point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:89
msgid "Build the Packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:91
msgid ""
"If you are building a release candidate and fast forwarded the ``release`` "
"branch the build server will already have started building it. If not, jump "
"in on the build server and trigger the Release/Syncthing job, for the "
"``release`` branch, while checking the options to rebuild all dependencies "
"in the chain. We need the rebuild for those binaries to pick up the new tag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:93
msgid ""
"Once the build succeeds, log in on ``secure.syncthing.net``. If something "
"failed in the build it's hopefully \"just\" a flaky test - redo the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:96
msgid "Create the GitHub release"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:98
msgid ""
"From this point on we will work on ``secure.s.n``, as the ``release`` user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:100
msgid ""
"jb@laptop$ ssh secure.syncthing.net\n"
"jb@secure$ sudo su - release\n"
"release@secure$"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:106
msgid ""
"We will use grt to create the release with the appropriate change log, and "
"possibly close the milestone. If we are doing a candidate release we need to "
"specify the tag including the candidate suffix:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:108
msgid "$ grt release v0.14.50-rc.1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:112
msgid ""
"This will create a v0.14.50-rc.1 release, with the \"pre-release\" bit set, "
"and leave the v0.14.50 milestone open. For a stable release:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:114
msgid "$ grt release v0.14.50"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:118
msgid "The milestone will be closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:121
msgid "Sign and upload the archives"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:123
msgid ""
"At this point the build should have completed and the artifacts should have "
"been uploaded to ``secure.s.n``. If the build number was 1234 and the "
"version v0.14.50 the files will be in ``/home/incoming/build-1234-"
"v0.14.50``. Run the following scripts. None of them should fail, barring "
"connectivity issues - so if they do, you get to fix whatever it is without "
"any guidance from me. Sorry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:125
msgid "$ sign-upload-debian /home/incoming/build-1234-v0.14.50"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:129
msgid "Publishes the Debian archives to apt.syncthing.net."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:131
msgid "$ upload-release /home/incoming/build-1234-v0.14.50"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:135
msgid "Publishes the regular release archives to GitHub."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:138
msgid "Stable Releases - Trigger update of the website"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:140
msgid ""
"The main website needs to be redeployed to reflect the new release version "
"on the download page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:142
msgid "$ ./deploy-web"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:147
msgid "Stable Releases - Create a post on the forum"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:149
msgid ""
"In the Announce/Releases category. Use the tag message as the template, make "
"the header a link to the release, make the issue numbers to be links to the "
"corresponding issues. You can use ``grt changelog v0.14.50 --md`` to get the "
"change log with issue links in proper Markdown."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:152
msgid "Stable Releases - Optionally, tweet it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:154
msgid ""
"If you have the Twitter account and the release isn't a cake-in-your-face "
"screwup fix that you'd rather no one ever heard about and want to just "
"silently roll out to everyone during the night."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:157
msgid "Stable Releases - Create new milestone"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:159
msgid ""
"Create a milestone for the next release, with the due date set to the first "
"Tuesday of the next month. Which version number to bump depends on what kind "
"of changes are already in main (see :ref:`semver`). This might change in the "
"time until the first candidate is released."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:165
msgid "Merge Release Into Main"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/release-creation.rst:167
msgid ""
"If this was a non-first candidate release with cherry picked commits on it, "
"merge ``release`` back into ``main`` and push ``main``."
msgstr ""
